workers have closed lanes adjacent to the demolition site

64
00:05:16,841 --> 00:05:19,660
but in just four hours

65
00:05:19,661 --> 00:05:21,940
they'll have to reopen them for the morning rush hour

66
00:05:21,941 --> 00:05:25,060
leaving a narrow window for the crew to finish its work

67
00:05:25,061 --> 00:05:30,040
the rest of the operation is going to go back tomorrow night

68
00:05:30,041 --> 00:05:38,700
We're going to all over start in the same process in to cutting, bringing it down and hauling it from the job site.

69
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:49,420
Although it may not look like it, the entire 23-mile project is 80% finished, with construction expected to end within one year.

70
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:56,940
The revamped highway is designed to absorb traffic increases through the corridor for the next two decades.

71
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:05,600
Once a sleepy farm town, Houston is now testament to the explosive growth generated by America's superhighway over the past half century.

72
00:06:07,180 --> 00:06:10,184
It all began in 1956, when President Dwight D.

73
00:06:10,185 --> 00:06:16,780
Eisenhower envisioned what would become the largest public works program in the history of the world.

74
00:06:16,781 --> 00:06:27,020
In a span of 40 years, construction crews moved 42 billion cubic yards of Earth,

75
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:31,140
10 times more than the volume extracted to create the Panama Canal.

76
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:36,780
Workers detonated 1 billion pounds of explosives,

77
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:39,940
mixed 2.3 billion tons of cement,

78
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:42,920
and placed millions of miles of steel rebar.

79
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:48,200
When the project was finally finished in 2007 with Boston's Big Dig,

80
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:55,300
they'd poured enough concrete to construct a wall nine feet thick and 50 feet high around the world.

81
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,580
The landscape of America was forever changed.

82
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:02,560
America is a great nation because of its highways.

83
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:05,840
It does not have highways because it's a great nation.

84
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:08,080
These highways make it what it is.

85
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:09,620
They make it wealthy.

86
00:07:10,220 --> 00:07:11,180
They make it powerful.

87
00:07:11,181 --> 00:07:12,640
They make it quick.

88
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:19,580
Today, motorists can reach the four corners of the United States by following numbered

89
00:07:19,581 --> 00:07:23,680
red, white and blue shields.

90
00:07:23,681 --> 00:07:28,180
Odd interstate numbers run north and south, with the larger numbers in the east and the

91
00:07:28,181 --> 00:07:31,580
smaller numbers in the west.

92
00:07:31,581 --> 00:07:36,120
East-west routes have even-numbered designations, with the larger numbers in the north and the

93
00:07:36,121 --> 00:07:39,660
smaller numbers in the south.

94
00:07:39,661 --> 00:07:44,760
In addition to these four transcontinental routes, there are an additional 209 interstate

95
00:07:44,761 --> 00:07:52,980
highways that web out across America's interior.

96
00:07:52,981 --> 00:07:54,129
While the U.S.

97
00:07:54,130 --> 00:08:00,780
interstate unifies the country, it also serves its intended purpose as a military

98
00:08:00,781 --> 00:08:01,780
defense network.

99
00:08:01,781 --> 00:08:05,780
Dwight Eisenhower obsessed about a nuclear holocaust.

100
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:10,780
It plagued him.

101
00:08:10,781 --> 00:08:13,780
And one of the things he wanted to do was deliver the interstate system,

102
00:08:13,781 --> 00:08:17,780
a superhighway network that would allow people, after a nuclear strike,

103
00:08:17,781 --> 00:08:21,780
to evacuate a city and then begin the rebuilding

104
00:08:21,781 --> 00:08:24,780
along those very roads that he evacuated on.

105
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:29,780
Eisenhower's worst fears never came to pass.

106
00:08:29,781 --> 00:08:34,240
But his interstate network has helped protect America in some of its darkest days.

107
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:41,140
September 11, 2001, all the planes around the United States were grounded.

108
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:45,460
What sustained us through that attack was the U.S. interstate system.

109
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:47,300
Trucks were sent to the airports.

110
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:50,040
Precious cargoes were unloaded from the bellies of the planes.

111
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:52,920
And life continued along the interstate system.

112
00:08:55,740 --> 00:08:59,460
And when Hurricane Katrina struck the Gulf Coast in 2005,

113
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:04,980
Over a million displaced residents found a path to safety along America's superhighways.

114
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:12,200
While the connectivity of America's interstate system helps protect the masses,

115
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:15,140
it's also created massive sprawl.

116
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:18,660
The interstate system is a victim of its own success.

117
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:20,380
It connects neighborhoods and cities,

118
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:24,760
but when it passes through a densely populated neighborhood, it creates a lot of noise.

119
00:09:26,220 --> 00:09:29,280
Surprisingly, the largest noise polluter isn't car engines.

120
00:09:29,780 --> 00:09:37,780
its car tires. 75% of highway noise is a result of tires rolling over the pavement.

121
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:44,960
The noise generates when tires push out air trapped beneath them, when they slip or stick

122
00:09:44,961 --> 00:09:48,500
to the road, or when they create suction with a road surface.

123
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:54,240
Directing sound barrier walls is one solution to dampen noise.

124
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:59,560
But since they cost a million dollars a mile, the search continues for alternatives.

125
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:05,480
Engineers at Purdue University are providing one with so-called quiet pavement.

126
00:10:06,680 --> 00:10:11,140
The rubber hits the road literally along this ring-shaped spinning treadmill.

127
00:10:12,620 --> 00:10:17,440
We put two microphones next to the tire and actually drive then across the road

128
00:10:17,441 --> 00:10:20,600
and measure how loud that particular pavement is.

129
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:24,720
The machine will go up to 30 miles an hour, and that's one revolution a second.

130
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:25,860
It's a pretty scary machine.

131
00:10:25,861 --> 00:10:29,420
I wouldn't be standing here with the machine going 30 miles an hour.

132
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:35,660
Safety reasons dictate the 30 mile per hour limit, but the recorded decibel levels are

133
00:10:35,661 --> 00:10:42,400
adjusted to represent noise at interstate speeds.

134
00:10:42,401 --> 00:10:47,380
To discover what types of road surface dampens car noise most effectively, Purdue's researchers

135
00:10:47,381 --> 00:10:51,500
test a variety of textured asphalt and concrete pavements.

136
00:10:51,501 --> 00:11:01,500
This pavement has texture to the surface, which gives channels for the air to escape.

137
00:11:01,501 --> 00:11:10,320
In the clapping mechanism, when the tire tread hits the pavement, there's air that would be compressed in a normal pavement.

138
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:14,860
In this pavement, it has a way to escape and get away from the tire.

139
00:11:14,861 --> 00:11:24,540
During testing, the operator cranks up the machine slowly to avoid any mishaps.

140
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:34,820
We're going up to 20 miles an hour, and I'm watching for anything flying off the machine.

141
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:43,080
At 30 miles an hour, depending on the weight of the object, it could come through the wall.

142
00:11:43,081 --> 00:11:47,000
If we lost that whole tire, that would be a good example.

143
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:51,360
So just how quiet is quiet pavement?

144
00:11:53,220 --> 00:11:58,560
There have been some recent studies where they've done surveys of a lot of the pavements in the country.

145
00:11:59,020 --> 00:12:03,460
And they've measured everything from about 103 decibels up to about 113 decibels.

146
00:12:03,700 --> 00:12:10,060
When we've been doing the quieter alternative, we're getting them from anywhere from about 104 decibels down to about 98 decibels.

147
00:12:10,061 --> 00:12:15,500
Although it may not seem like much, that's enough for a driver to notice.

148
00:12:18,060 --> 00:12:23,600
Loud or quiet, America's interstate system is the gold standard for superhighways throughout the world.

