00:19:10,521 --> 00:19:21,520
To build this massive interchange, workers mixed 350,000 cubic yards of concrete, planted 710 bridge collars,

230
00:19:21,521 --> 00:19:26,740
placed 31 miles of bridge beams and bored 2 million square feet of bridge deck.

231
00:19:29,160 --> 00:19:34,920
Yet the greatest challenge for engineers was constructing the Hi-5 over the footprint of the old interchange

232
00:19:34,921 --> 00:19:37,420
without turning the highway into a parking lot.

233
00:19:38,380 --> 00:19:45,320
I would compare it to living in your house and building a new house around the house while you're living in it.

234
00:19:45,380 --> 00:19:47,680
Now, it's insane, but that's what we had to do.

235
00:19:47,681 --> 00:19:54,680
Placing the high five support piers and columns was the crew's first order of business.

236
00:19:54,681 --> 00:20:01,680
Drivers hold large precast segments from a neighboring casting yard to the construction site.

237
00:20:01,681 --> 00:20:09,680
Erecting them conventionally using large cranes would have taken up too large a footprint in the confined work area.

238
00:20:09,681 --> 00:20:13,680
So engineers relied on the brute strength of an Italian import.

239
00:20:13,681 --> 00:20:21,880
The contractor came up with an innovative idea to build a special piece of equipment we called Sergio.

240
00:20:22,280 --> 00:20:27,480
Sergio was a segment erector to take the segments of precast and load them up onto the bridge.

241
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:32,300
And this required very little manpower, basically, to get it done.

242
00:20:34,520 --> 00:20:41,360
Safely attached to the top of the bridge pier, Sergio hoisted the 75-ton concrete segments into position.

243
00:20:43,680 --> 00:20:54,540
After Sergio lifts a segment, it rotates it on its axis, moves it laterally, then lowers it into position.

244
00:20:56,340 --> 00:20:59,500
Then workers secure the segment to the existing bridge pier.

245
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:12,120
After four years of construction, the $270 million Dallas High Five officially opened in December 2005, one year ahead of schedule.

246
00:21:12,900 --> 00:21:15,420
Today, it's far more than just a towering interchange.

247
00:21:16,420 --> 00:21:18,840
The High Five is basically a landmark.

248
00:21:19,040 --> 00:21:21,580
In fact, it's a destination for tourists to the area.

249
00:21:22,140 --> 00:21:25,640
We have individuals that are becoming famous for taking photographs of the interchange.

250
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:28,960
It's on the cover right now of the Dallas telephone book.

251
00:21:29,900 --> 00:21:33,760
And don't be surprised if similar colossal interchanges start popping up

252
00:21:33,761 --> 00:21:36,040
in other densely populated cities across America.

253
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:40,980
Unfortunately, it may be even bigger in the future for some of these other projects,

254
00:21:40,981 --> 00:21:46,180
And if people are scared about the high five now, I can't imagine what they're going to say when it's high seven or high nine.

255
00:21:47,380 --> 00:21:49,260
They might just start selling tickets.

256
00:21:50,740 --> 00:21:57,920
While stack interchanges help keep traffic flowing, grueling commutes still plague America's superhighway.

257
00:21:58,560 --> 00:22:04,240
One motorist finally lowered the boom on a bridge that bedeviled him for years.

258
00:22:04,241 --> 00:22:14,920
The fastest superhighways in the United States are I-10 and I-20 in rural western Texas,

259
00:22:14,921 --> 00:22:20,500
where drivers can lawfully speed up to 80 miles an hour, as long as there's daylight.

260
00:22:21,700 --> 00:22:26,840
Superhighways will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

261
00:22:28,020 --> 00:22:31,600
We now return to Superhighways on Modern Marvels.

262
00:22:34,240 --> 00:22:39,160
The extraordinary success of America's superhighway comes at a price.

263
00:22:40,320 --> 00:22:46,480
The freedom to travel to the four corners of the country has created the largest car club in the world.

264
00:22:47,080 --> 00:22:51,000
People live in their cars. They commute in their cars. They vacation in their cars.

265
00:22:51,600 --> 00:22:57,080
Today, Americans are traveling further than they ever have to get from home to work.

266
00:22:58,780 --> 00:23:03,820
In the United States, 80% of adult Americans own a licensed car.

267
00:23:04,240 --> 00:23:11,120
that's 247 million drivers and it's creating widespread traffic jams across

268
00:23:11,121 --> 00:23:14,240
the US interstate system

269
00:23:15,240 --> 00:23:21,960
you stay meet Dan Ruffley a battle-hardened road warrior who's about

270
00:23:21,961 --> 00:23:27,960
to embark on his morning commute like a lot of us he hates the ritual but he was

271
00:23:27,961 --> 00:23:30,060
destined to dish out some sweet revenge.

272
00:23:32,020 --> 00:23:38,480
My God, I've been driving this commute for probably about 38 years.

273
00:23:40,100 --> 00:23:46,680
Dan's 45-mile commute from Acoqueque, Maryland, takes him along I-95 and the Woodrow-Wilson

274
00:23:46,681 --> 00:23:47,381
Bridge.

275
00:23:49,440 --> 00:23:55,660
The Woodrow-Wilson Bridge connects the states of Maryland and Virginia, spanning the Potomac

276
00:23:55,661 --> 00:23:56,361
River.

277
00:23:56,362 --> 00:24:02,400
here I-95 shrinks from four lanes to three and merges with several other

278
00:24:02,401 --> 00:24:09,580
roadway arteries creating one of the worst traffic bottlenecks in America to

279
00:24:09,581 --> 00:24:13,320
make matters worse it's also one of the few drawbridges on the US interstate

280
00:24:13,321 --> 00:24:22,300
system dance frustration with the bridge hit a wall in 1999 during an

281
00:24:22,301 --> 00:24:23,980
especially painful morning commute.

282
00:24:24,580 --> 00:24:29,240
And I was going to work one morning early, about 4.30 in the morning,

283
00:24:29,980 --> 00:24:34,440
and changed lanes and ran into the back of a tractor trailer that was parked,

284
00:24:34,580 --> 00:24:37,380
half on the bridge and half on the shoulder.

285
00:24:37,640 --> 00:24:43,140
And after the ambulance got me out and started to move to go across the bridge

286
00:24:43,141 --> 00:24:49,140
to come back to the hospital, moved about 50 feet, they opened the draw span of the bridge.

287
00:24:49,141 --> 00:24:55,840
And from the time we started to move, it took probably about 45 minutes to an hour to get to the hospital.

288
00:24:57,320 --> 00:25:04,400
In 2006, after nearly four decades of commuter headaches, Dan finally got a chance to strike back.

289
00:25:04,401 --> 00:25:18,401
He was awarded the honor of blowing up his concrete nemesis by a multi-agency project

290
00:25:19,260 --> 00:25:22,020
created to rebuild the Woodrow Wilson Bridge.

291
00:25:25,180 --> 00:25:30,880
Today, the new dual-span bridge is roughly 70% complete, enough to handle the daily traffic

292
00:25:30,881 --> 00:25:31,600
across the river.

293
00:25:32,300 --> 00:25:37,820
For Dan and the 200,000 other commuters that pass here each day, the future looks a lot wider.

294
00:25:38,980 --> 00:25:44,140
The new Woodrow Wilson Bridge will have six lanes in each direction, twice as many lanes as the old bridge.

295
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:50,980
This will quadruple its daily capacity from 75,000 vehicles to 300,000.

296
00:25:52,760 --> 00:25:55,820
Yet for aesthetic reasons, it will remain a drawbridge.

297
00:25:56,480 --> 00:26:02,040
If we would have gone to a high bridge, it would have been 135 feet high over the middle of the river,

298
00:26:02,520 --> 00:26:08,480
and it would have been an eyesore compared to the rest of the monuments in downtown Washington, D.C.

299
00:26:12,380 --> 00:26:18,400
Sitting atop graceful arched piers, the new draw span towers 70 feet higher than its predecessor,

300
00:26:18,820 --> 00:26:23,480
which should decrease the number of openings by five times, to about 50 per year.

301
00:26:23,481 --> 00:26:29,760
Erecting this new span of interstate requires huge amounts of concrete and steel.

302
00:26:30,500 --> 00:26:35,280
We have over 34 acres of bridge deck on this project,

303
00:26:35,820 --> 00:26:41,100
2,500 miles of rebar in the deck, and over 80 million pounds of structural steel.

304
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,820
While the southbound span of the bridge is nearly finished,

305
00:26:46,300 --> 00:26:49,000
it'll take another year to get the northbound span up and running.

306
00:26:49,001 --> 00:26:54,000
Still, the new bridge is already winning the praise of one of its harshest critics.

307
00:26:54,001 --> 00:27:00,000
It's still a bottleneck because the other six lanes aren't open yet, but the openings

308
00:27:00,001 --> 00:27:05,000
have been a big improvement on the traffic.

309
00:27:05,001 --> 00:27:11,000
While an open drawbridge will certainly put the brakes on your commute, a single distracted

310
00:27:11,001 --> 00:27:17,000
driver can have a surprisingly detrimental impact on a superhighway's ebb and flow.

311
00:27:17,001 --> 00:27:21,500
Just ask MIT engineers in Cambridge, Massachusetts,

312
00:27:21,501 --> 00:27:25,500
who've developed a sophisticated piece of civil engineering software

313
00:27:25,501 --> 00:27:27,500
that studies highway traffic patterns.

314
00:27:27,501 --> 00:27:32,500
Each vehicle here is characterized by certain behavior patterns,

315
00:27:32,501 --> 00:27:35,500
like how aggressive they are when they merge into lanes,

316
00:27:35,501 --> 00:27:38,500
how much of a desired speed that they would like to maintain,

317
00:27:38,501 --> 00:27:40,500
and the effect of all these individual behaviors

318
00:27:40,501 --> 00:27:44,500
is what you would see as the resulting traffic pattern on the network.

319
00:27:44,501 --> 00:27:50,780
Lowering the desired speed of one vehicle by half causes a dramatic rippling impact on traffic,

320
00:27:51,780 --> 00:27:55,460
demonstrating how one distracted driver can clog a superhighway artery.

321
00:27:56,460 --> 00:28:03,100
So now we are zooming out a little bit to see what impact that single slowdown vehicle had on the rest of the traffic.

322
00:28:03,680 --> 00:28:06,740
One distracted driver is all it takes to shut down the entire freeway.

323
00:28:06,741 --> 00:28:14,900
real life traffic delays force american commuters to waste 4.2 billion hours a year in their vehicles

324
00:28:14,901 --> 00:28:22,640
in the car mecca of the united states southern california each driver spends an average of 72

325
00:28:22,641 --> 00:28:28,900
hours a year sitting in traffic here at the caltrans traffic management center in los angeles

326
00:28:28,901 --> 00:28:32,500
keeping vehicles moving is an unenviable task.

327
00:28:33,580 --> 00:28:40,380
We're trying to keep traffic moving in probably the most congested urban area in the world.

328
00:28:40,940 --> 00:28:46,280
We have 100 million vehicle miles driven every day,

329
00:28:47,080 --> 00:28:51,320
and that's like circumventing the globe 2,000 times.

330
00:28:52,960 --> 00:28:56,180
Using electronic sensors and electronic eyes,

331
00:28:56,760 --> 00:29:00,200
operators monitor for traffic accidents that can quickly tie up the system.

332
00:29:00,980 --> 00:29:04,080
This is one of our closed-circuit TV cameras here.

333
00:29:04,400 --> 00:29:08,880
This particular one is on the 101 freeway in the Hollywood area,

334
00:29:09,020 --> 00:29:13,860
and as you can see, heading northbound on the 101, it's starting to get congested now.

335
00:29:13,861 --> 00:29:17,000
On a route like this, if a car were to break down,

336
00:29:17,340 --> 00:29:22,100
it's that much more important for us to get whatever the problem is out of the way there.

337
00:29:23,760 --> 00:29:26,020
As soon as operators discover a problem,

338
00:29:26,180 --> 00:29:31,620
the traffic management center disperses the appropriate response team speed is key

339
00:29:38,660 --> 00:29:43,540
each minute that a vehicle remains disabled in a lane creates seven minutes in traffic delays

340
00:29:46,340 --> 00:29:51,620
while being stuck in traffic can be a drag it's not quite as bad when you've got a view like this

341
00:29:51,621 --> 00:29:59,120
These may be the most scenic superhighways in the world, but building them was anything

342
00:29:59,121 --> 00:30:03,040
but paradise.

343
00:30:03,041 --> 00:30:07,580
According to a published report by the Texas Transportation Institute, traffic delays in

344
00:30:07,581 --> 00:30:10,123
the United States cost the U.S.

345
00:30:10,124 --> 00:30:14,800
economy $78.2 billion a year, more than triple the amount

346
00:30:14,801 --> 00:30:21,140
allotted to build the interstate highway system in 1956.

347
00:30:21,141 --> 00:30:25,320
will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

348
00:30:27,780 --> 00:30:31,320
We now return to Superhighways on Modern Marvels.

349
00:30:33,220 --> 00:30:36,934
There's a good reason why so many of us find driving along the U.S.

350
00:30:36,935 --> 00:30:38,320
interstate system boring.

351
00:30:39,060 --> 00:30:45,320
From its straightaways to its curves to its signs, it's utterly predictable.

352
00:30:46,100 --> 00:30:48,020
But that's part of the secret of its success.

353
00:30:48,760 --> 00:30:53,540
No matter where you are in America, these interstate highway lanes are all the same.

354
00:30:53,880 --> 00:30:58,280
Four lanes of traffic at least, traveling in two different directions, 12-foot travel lanes,

355
00:30:58,560 --> 00:31:02,820
a 10-foot breakdown lane on your right, a 3-foot breakdown lane on your left.

356
00:31:03,160 --> 00:31:06,760
All of the signs are identical so that no matter where you are,

357
00:31:07,200 --> 00:31:09,960
your eye is trained to pick up what it needs to do.

358
00:31:11,860 --> 00:31:14,049
Before construction began on the U.S.

359
00:31:14,050 --> 00:31:17,600
interstate, drivers traveled on two-lane undivided highways.

360
00:31:18,020 --> 00:31:21,900
An ill-advised lane change could easily trigger a head-on collision.

361
00:31:23,160 --> 00:31:27,800
In the 1950s, there was an average of 50,000 driver fatalities per year.

362
00:31:29,260 --> 00:31:34,560
One of America's deadliest highways was Route 6, across Colorado's Rocky Mountains.

363
00:31:35,540 --> 00:31:41,160
These mountains were filled with precipitous drop-offs, sometimes 1,000-foot cliffs with no guardrails.

364
00:31:41,340 --> 00:31:43,520
These roads were death waiting to happen.

365
00:31:43,521 --> 00:31:50,240
today motorists rely on the safer more predictable interstate 70 to carry them

366
00:31:50,241 --> 00:31:57,680
through these same mountains via the Eisenhower tunnel and then west across a

367
00:31:57,681 --> 00:32:05,600
grand section of superhighway I 70 through Glenwood Canyon

368
00:32:05,601 --> 00:32:11,960
the picturesque 12-mile stretch of I 70 features 40 massive bridges three

369
00:32:11,961 --> 00:32:18,360
gigantic tunnels and a sophisticated control center that's got its own private entrance.

370
00:32:22,440 --> 00:32:27,880
Yet perhaps its most spectacular engineering feat is how well it blends in with the grandeur

371
00:32:27,881 --> 00:32:29,113
of the landscape.

372
00:32:29,114 --> 00:32:34,700
This interstate looks like it sort of fell from the sky and somehow landed in

373
00:32:34,701 --> 00:32:36,024
the bottom of the canyon.

374
00:32:36,025 --> 00:32:39,680
When you're driving through, you see a lot of native plants. You also

375
00:32:39,681 --> 00:32:42,132
see a canyon that looks very natural.

376
00:32:42,133 --> 00:32:45,380
The seamless integration of such a colossal piece

377
00:32:45,381 --> 00:32:48,465
of infrastructure didn't happen by accident.

378
00:32:48,466 --> 00:32:52,180
It was a top priority for engineers when construction

379
00:32:52,181 --> 00:32:53,646
began in 1982.

380
00:32:53,647 --> 00:33:01,080
Their first task was deciding how to squeeze I-70's four lanes over the

381
00:33:01,081 --> 00:33:04,846
existing two-lane footprint of Route 6.

382
00:33:04,847 --> 00:33:09,480
The answer was to build an efficient split-level

383
00:33:09,481 --> 00:33:10,181
highway.

384
00:33:10,182 --> 00:33:15,800
That really became one of our primary design kind of constraints was trying to stay

385
00:33:15,801 --> 00:33:20,520
within that disturbed area and that terraced alignment concept allows us to put the westbound

386
00:33:20,521 --> 00:33:25,620
lane up on the upper deck, the eastbound lane on the lower deck and then using retaining walls and

387
00:33:25,621 --> 00:33:29,246
squeezing it so that we could get it in that disturbed area.

388
00:33:29,247 --> 00:33:31,120
Wherever the highway threatened

389
00:33:31,121 --> 00:33:37,540
Glenwood Canyon's ecological treasures, engineers erected graceful viaducts using precast concrete

390
00:33:37,541 --> 00:33:45,151
segments. At a facility 20 miles away, workers poured the 36-ton segments.

391
00:33:45,152 --> 00:33:46,900
To lift them into

392
00:33:46,901 --> 00:33:52,660
place, engineers called upon a machine similar to the Dallas Hi-5 Sergio in order to protect

