00:33:52,661 --> 00:33:54,907
the fragile floor of the canyon.

394
00:33:54,908 --> 00:33:58,980
The erection device we used was called an erection gantry.

395
00:33:58,981 --> 00:34:05,060
Well, the erection gantry is really a crane that sits up on the bridge, and what that did was it

396
00:34:05,061 --> 00:34:09,540
replaced a more conventional like crawler crane that would be sitting down on the ground

397
00:34:09,541 --> 00:34:15,040
underneath the bridge. Sitting atop a partially completed viaduct,

398
00:34:15,700 --> 00:34:19,912
the erection gantry hoisted the precast segment into place.

399
00:34:19,913 --> 00:34:22,340
Once in position, workers attached

400
00:34:22,341 --> 00:34:28,102
its bracketed edge to the bridge span, securing it with epoxy and steel tensioning rods.

401
00:34:28,103 --> 00:34:28,803
Once it

402
00:34:28,804 --> 00:34:33,653
was secured, the erection gantry repeated the process on the opposite side.

403
00:34:33,654 --> 00:34:34,740
There's a lot of

404
00:34:34,741 --> 00:34:39,340
benefit of being able to build these types of bridges from above so that you can protect

405
00:34:39,341 --> 00:34:41,448
the vegetation features underneath.

406
00:34:41,449 --> 00:34:44,940
And in this case, there's also an active stream underneath

407
00:34:44,941 --> 00:34:47,600
that wasn't touched at all as part of this construction.

408
00:34:49,680 --> 00:34:56,560
The meticulous construction of I-70 through Glenwood Canyon took 12 long years and $490

409
00:34:56,561 --> 00:34:58,315
million to complete.

410
00:34:58,316 --> 00:35:04,720
Today, it provides motorists with safe passage through and stunning views

411
00:35:04,721 --> 00:35:08,084
of one of nature's greatest masterpieces.

412
00:35:08,085 --> 00:35:11,940
To discover a stretch of interstate that rivals

413
00:35:11,941 --> 00:35:17,400
the eco-friendly design of Glenwood Canyon, you'll have to travel thousands of miles across

414
00:35:17,401 --> 00:35:21,804
the Pacific Ocean to the Hawaiian island of Oahu.

415
00:35:21,805 --> 00:35:25,040
Here the term interstate is a bit of

416
00:35:25,041 --> 00:35:28,040
a misnomer. So why on earth is it here?

417
00:35:28,041 --> 00:35:34,040
That's a question I've always gotten from everybody that I've spoken to. They say,

418
00:35:34,041 --> 00:35:37,880
It's an interstate highway, but it doesn't go to any other state.

419
00:35:37,881 --> 00:35:43,760
And I remind them that when President Dwight Eisenhower signed that interstate bill in,

420
00:35:43,761 --> 00:35:49,920
it was called and still is the Interstate and Defense Highway Network.

421
00:35:49,921 --> 00:35:54,740
The island of Oahu is home to all three of Hawaii's interstate highways.

422
00:35:54,741 --> 00:36:02,940
Each carries its own unique interstate designation, H1, H2, and H3.

423
00:36:02,941 --> 00:36:09,020
a 16-mile, mostly elevated highway, linking the Honolulu area to the Kaneohe Marine Corps

424
00:36:09,021 --> 00:36:17,138
Air Base. H3 is arguably the most stunning superhighway in the world.

425
00:36:17,139 --> 00:36:19,020
But for workers,

426
00:36:19,021 --> 00:36:24,129
it was an arduous venture that pitted them against unforgiving terrain.

427
00:36:24,130 --> 00:36:25,280
Here, five miles

428
00:36:25,281 --> 00:36:31,660
west of Honolulu in the dense jungle of the Halawa Valley, workers began construction

429
00:36:31,661 --> 00:36:35,000
on the $1.3 billion H3 in 1986.

430
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:38,720
This whole valley was, as you see it here on the sides,

431
00:36:39,240 --> 00:36:40,020
nothing but trees.

432
00:36:40,100 --> 00:36:42,920
In fact, we had a difficult time getting up.

433
00:36:43,380 --> 00:36:47,200
I had one of our engineers up there with the head bulldozer,

434
00:36:47,640 --> 00:36:50,420
and he just pointed out which way he wanted them to go,

435
00:36:50,421 --> 00:36:53,660
and they just bulldozed out the vegetation.

436
00:36:55,020 --> 00:36:56,860
Since the valley was prone to flooding,

437
00:36:57,420 --> 00:37:01,280
engineers raised this portion of H3 onto a six-mile-long viaduct.

438
00:37:01,660 --> 00:37:11,660
Past the viaduct, H3's route hits the face of a massive obstacle, the 3,000-foot-high Ko'olau mountain range.

439
00:37:11,661 --> 00:37:19,660
Here, engineers would have to tunnel through volcanic rock to construct H3's inbound and outbound twin tunnels.

440
00:37:19,661 --> 00:37:25,660
But hidden inside the mountain lurked a potential danger.

441
00:37:25,661 --> 00:37:30,100
Engineers were concerned that the heavy rainfall on this part of Oahu, which can

442
00:37:30,101 --> 00:37:34,500
total up to 200 inches a year, had left pockets of water trapped in the mountain.

443
00:37:34,501 --> 00:37:40,660
Accidentally drilling through one could cause a massive flood that would force

444
00:37:40,661 --> 00:37:46,260
engineers to change the tunnels course. An exploratory tunnel was carved to

445
00:37:46,261 --> 00:37:52,520
determine a safe elevation for the twin bores. Today it serves as a maintenance

446
00:37:52,521 --> 00:37:53,221
portal.

447
00:37:53,222 --> 00:37:58,920
Here we are in the exploratory bore off of the exploratory tunnel and the main concern is

448
00:37:58,921 --> 00:37:59,621
water.

449
00:37:59,622 --> 00:38:05,780
There are dike compartments, very dense rock that is vertical and sometimes the water

450
00:38:05,781 --> 00:38:09,553
gets confined in this like a big standing pool of water.

451
00:38:09,554 --> 00:38:12,180
So when we're drilling a tunnel through

452
00:38:12,181 --> 00:38:16,950
this kind of geology, if we hit that water, we've got a problem.

453
00:38:16,951 --> 00:38:19,260
To avoid trapped water pockets,

454
00:38:19,261 --> 00:38:23,640
engineers cut the nearly mile-long tunnels along a 1,000-foot incline.

455
00:38:24,780 --> 00:38:26,880
Fortunately, they never found water.

456
00:38:27,520 --> 00:38:30,160
But as the tunnel empties out at its highest vantage point,

457
00:38:30,660 --> 00:38:35,460
drivers discover a breathtaking view of the Haiku Valley along this majestic viaduct.

458
00:38:36,120 --> 00:38:38,220
And it's got beautiful curves in it.

459
00:38:38,640 --> 00:38:43,020
This particular section of the highway has won national and international awards.

460
00:38:44,520 --> 00:38:48,980
No matter where America's 46,000 miles of superhighways extend,

461
00:38:49,260 --> 00:38:54,900
An army of road warriors must learn how to cope with the chaos that can erupt on them at any time.

462
00:38:55,860 --> 00:38:59,380
And there's a lot more to their training method than fun and games.

463
00:39:02,360 --> 00:39:10,040
The rugged terrain of Oahu's North Halawa Valley was so remote that helicopters carried surveyors to the H-3 work site.

464
00:39:10,840 --> 00:39:16,400
At the end of each day's work, the surveyors built a makeshift landing pad so they could be picked up.

465
00:39:16,401 --> 00:39:23,400
Superhighways will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

466
00:39:23,401 --> 00:39:29,400
We now return to Superhighways on Modern Marvels.

467
00:39:29,401 --> 00:39:38,400
At least every three miles on America's Superhighway is a familiar green and white sign directing drivers to their destinations.

468
00:39:38,401 --> 00:39:45,400
But when the interstate was first being designed, the sign's now famous color scheme was a point of contention.

469
00:39:45,401 --> 00:39:53,100
When they launched the construction of the interstate system, one Federal Highway Administrator was in charge of picking out the colors of the signs.

470
00:39:53,700 --> 00:39:56,180
And he was bent on blue and white.

471
00:39:56,520 --> 00:39:58,840
But the problem was, he was colorblind.

472
00:39:59,340 --> 00:40:01,640
He didn't know it, and no one who worked for him knew it.

473
00:40:01,940 --> 00:40:08,060
So they built a stretch of road, put people and cars on it, and let them pass through all these different colors of signs.

474
00:40:08,860 --> 00:40:13,400
The administrator was overruled overwhelmingly, and the signs are now green and white.

475
00:40:13,401 --> 00:40:15,920
thankfully because we can see them much better at night.

476
00:40:18,080 --> 00:40:20,820
Today, the easily discernible green and white signs

477
00:40:20,821 --> 00:40:23,000
give drivers traveling at appropriate speeds

478
00:40:23,001 --> 00:40:25,240
10 seconds to read and react,

479
00:40:25,980 --> 00:40:28,320
deterring them from making last-second lane changes

480
00:40:28,321 --> 00:40:29,460
that can trigger accidents.

481
00:40:31,380 --> 00:40:34,400
There are 2.5 million accidents each year

482
00:40:34,401 --> 00:40:35,340
on America's highways,

483
00:40:35,940 --> 00:40:39,100
and more than half occur in inclement weather.

484
00:40:40,720 --> 00:40:42,740
Many result from black ice,

485
00:40:42,741 --> 00:40:45,920
a thin layer of road ice that's virtually undetectable.

486
00:40:46,960 --> 00:40:49,680
But a new technology may soon change that.

487
00:40:51,080 --> 00:40:54,200
Cargill Safe Lane is a revolutionary pavement overlay

488
00:40:54,201 --> 00:40:58,920
made of a sponge-like aggregate that can store any chemical de-icing compound.

489
00:41:00,980 --> 00:41:03,120
Applied on pavement in non-freezing temperatures,

490
00:41:03,780 --> 00:41:07,180
the de-icing chemical is released when temperatures drop below freezing,

491
00:41:08,240 --> 00:41:09,920
preventing the road from icing over.

492
00:41:09,921 --> 00:41:14,780
Of course, accidents occur in good weather, too.

493
00:41:15,780 --> 00:41:23,980
And removing stricken vehicles safely from lanes rests on the shoulders of firefighters, policemen, EMTs, and tow truck drivers.

494
00:41:24,460 --> 00:41:26,500
They're called emergency first responders.

495
00:41:28,580 --> 00:41:37,160
Here at the University of Maryland Center for Advanced Transportation Technology, a one-of-a-kind first responder training simulation is in progress.

496
00:41:37,161 --> 00:41:38,891
This is communications.

497
00:41:38,892 --> 00:41:43,858
There is a multi-car accident on I-95 southbound at mile marker 2.

498
00:41:43,859 --> 00:41:45,740
EMT support is requested.

499
00:41:46,820 --> 00:41:56,960
Here, we're training the folks that manage and operate the road to do their jobs in the most efficient manner to keep the traffic moving along the interstate system.

500
00:41:57,740 --> 00:42:00,380
I copy one priority one. What's the location of that patient?

501
00:42:01,420 --> 00:42:05,260
This first-person simulation game may look like the latest video game release.

502
00:42:05,261 --> 00:42:09,420
but it's actually a sophisticated piece of traffic management software.

503
00:42:10,480 --> 00:42:15,660
Each person that plays the game has his own avatar that pulls them into the heart of the action.

504
00:42:16,600 --> 00:42:19,480
Both those patients you were just talking about, are they from this black SUV here?

505
00:42:20,580 --> 00:42:23,160
By creating a realistic virtual traffic jam,

506
00:42:23,720 --> 00:42:28,900
first responders gain invaluable experience that before could only be learned on the job.

507
00:42:30,540 --> 00:42:34,420
Amidst all this virtual chaos, the goal of the simulation is clear.

508
00:42:35,260 --> 00:42:42,140
If they take too long to deal with the incident, they see the queues building up.

509
00:42:42,600 --> 00:42:45,740
If they do the right thing, they see traffic flowing better.

510
00:42:46,180 --> 00:42:50,340
They get a greater sense of how their actions affect the real world.

511
00:42:51,020 --> 00:42:53,100
This is communications. Tow trucks are on their way.

512
00:42:54,240 --> 00:43:00,200
Quickly clearing accident scenes and keeping traffic flowing not only saves America billions of dollars,

513
00:43:00,480 --> 00:43:04,860
but also helps prevent subsequent crashes at the site that could shut down the highway.

514
00:43:05,260 --> 00:43:18,140
Statistics have shown that for every one minute that a lane is blocked on the roadway, the chances of a secondary collision occurring goes up 3%.

515
00:43:18,141 --> 00:43:23,100
And oftentimes that secondary collision is far worse than the first collision.

516
00:43:23,900 --> 00:43:27,080
This simulation game is still in its early development stages.

517
00:43:27,820 --> 00:43:31,860
But these first responders already recognize its unique value as a training tool.

518
00:43:31,861 --> 00:43:37,860
In my first accident scene, I was definitely a little nervous, was a little unsure.

519
00:43:37,861 --> 00:43:41,860
I think this tool would be great in any academy to have them go through these scenarios.

520
00:43:41,861 --> 00:43:46,860
That way they'll have the experience when they actually get on the road and face these real-life situations.

521
00:43:46,861 --> 00:43:51,860
Making the world's highways smarter, as well as the people that manage them,

522
00:43:51,861 --> 00:43:56,860
is just one factor that will keep traffic flowing smoothly in decades to come.

523
00:43:56,861 --> 00:44:03,860
The superhighways of tomorrow will be everything that our superhighways are today, but more.

524
00:44:03,861 --> 00:44:08,860
More electronics, more lanes, more modes of transportation along those corridors,

525
00:44:08,861 --> 00:44:17,860
trains linking up with highways, linking up with seaports, and even airports to move goods to and from.

526
00:44:17,861 --> 00:44:24,860
As long as the gateway to economic prosperity is lined in concrete and asphalt,

527
00:44:24,861 --> 00:44:31,860
superhighways will continue to expand farther, stand taller,

528
00:44:31,861 --> 00:44:38,860
and stretch wider than any man-made structures in the world.

